          "support": "(Inset) Transient change in spectral weight as a function of incident pump fluence at t=3 ps."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 84,
          "line_end": 84,
          "support": "The population inversion picture is further verified by the incident pump fluence dependence of the negative spectral weight, -ΔSW, which follows a threshold-saturation behavior expected for population inversion between excited energy levels, as shown in the inset of Fig. 3(f) and Supplemental Material section S6."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "delta_E_over_E",
      "definition": "Pump-induced change in the reflected THz electric field at the peak value divided by the peak value of the equilibrium THz waveform.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Peak value of the THz waveform",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 68,
          "line_end": 68,
          "support": "Here, ΔE is the pump-induced change in the reflected THz field at the peak value, and E is the peak value of the equilibrium THz waveform."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "delta_sigma_1",
      "definition": "Real part of the pump-induced change in complex optical conductivity, Δσ1(ω) = Re[σ_transient(ω) - σ_equilibrium(ω)].",
      "unit": "arbitrary units",
      "statistic": "Frequency-resolved values",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "Figure 3(a-d) shows the time evolution of the light-induced change in the complex optical conductivity, defined as Δσ(ω) = σ(ω)^transient - σ(ω)^equilibrium. At the maximum response (Fig. 3(a)), Δσ1(ω) is positive at low energies and becomes negative in between 6 and 10 meV, while Δσ2(ω) is positive over the entire energy window."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "delta_sigma_2",
      "definition": "Imaginary part of the pump-induced change in complex optical conductivity, Δσ2(ω) = Im[σ_transient(ω) - σ_equilibrium(ω)].",
      "unit": "arbitrary units",
      "statistic": "Frequency-resolved values",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "Figure 3(a-d) shows the time evolution of the light-induced change in the complex optical conductivity, defined as Δσ(ω) = σ(ω)^transient - σ(ω)^equilibrium. At the maximum response (Fig. 3(a)), Δσ1(ω) is positive at low energies and becomes negative in between 6 and 10 meV, while Δσ2(ω) is positive over the entire energy window."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "delta_spectral_weight",
      "definition": "Transient spectral weight defined as ΔSW = (120/π) ∫_{ω1}^{ω2} Δσ1(ω) dω, integrated between 4 and 10 meV.",
      "unit": "arbitrary units",
      "statistic": "Integrated over frequency range 4-10 meV",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "The transient spectral weight, defined as ΔSW=120/π ∫_{ω1}^{ω2} Δσ1(ω) dω, in the range between 4 and 10 meV (open squares in Fig. 3(f)) qualitatively follows the exponential fit for the spectrally-integrated response presented in Fig. 2(a)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "drude_oscillator_strength",
      "definition": "Oscillator strength of the Drude term in the fit to Δσ(ω), denoted ω_pD^2.",
      "unit": "arbitrary units",
      "statistic": "Time-dependent fitting parameter",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "Figure 3(f) plots the time evolution of the Drude and Lorentz oscillator strengths. The Drude oscillator strength ω_pD^2 can be fit with a single exponential decay with a time constant of 2.4 ps."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "inverted_lorentz_oscillator_strength",
      "definition": "Oscillator strength of the inverted Lorentz term in the fit to Δσ(ω), denoted ω_pL^2.",
      "unit": "arbitrary units",
      "statistic": "Time-dependent fitting parameter",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "Figure 3(f) plots the time evolution of the Drude and Lorentz oscillator strengths. ... The inverted Lorentz oscillator strength ω_pL^2 reaches maximum at 2 ps then decays with 2 ps and 15 ps time constants."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "long_lived_negative_response_peakI",
      "statement": "With resonant pumping at peak I (1.476 eV) at 7 K, the pump-probe signal ΔE/E quickly switches sign and remains negative for several tens of picoseconds, with a long-lived negative signal lifetime of 17 ps.",
      "quantitative_form": "ΔE/E(t) = A_short * exp(-t / 0.2 ps) + A_long * exp(-t / 17 ps) + magnon oscillation with 1.2 THz and 70 ps decay",
      "mechanism_ids": ["transient_response_lifetimes"],
      "control_names": ["pump_photon_energy", "temperature"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_E_over_E"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["base_temperature_7K", "pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Resonant pumping at 1.476 eV at 7 K in the antiferromagnetic state of NiPS3.",
      "statistical_basis": "Fitting over an extended time window (Fig. S3) reveals two time constants.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 68,
          "line_end": 68,
          "support": "Figure 2(a) shows the time-domain pump-probe response at 7 K with resonant excitation at peak I. ... After excitation, the pump-probe signal reaches maximum and then decreases to negative values, remaining negative for several tens of picoseconds. Fitting over an extended time window (Fig. S3) reveals two time constants of the transient response. The sign-changing response is short-lived with a lifetime of 0.2 ps, which is likely the time of exciton formation. The long-lived negative signal has a lifetime of 17 ps, which is on the same order as the lifetime of the exciton."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "positive_short_lived_off_resonant",
      "statement": "When pumping at peak II (1.494 eV) or above the absorption edge (1.610 eV) at 7 K, the pump-probe signals are nearly identical, showing only a positive response with a lifetime on the order of 3 ps.",
      "quantitative_form": "ΔE/E(t) = A * exp(-t / 3 ps) with positive amplitude",
      "mechanism_ids": ["transient_response_lifetimes"],
      "control_names": ["pump_photon_energy", "temperature"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_E_over_E"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["base_temperature_7K", "pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Pumping at 1.494 eV or 1.610 eV at 7 K.",
      "statistical_basis": "Comparison of time traces at two pump energies.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 70,
          "line_end": 70,
          "support": "As illustrated in Fig. 2(b), when pumping at peak II (1.494 eV) or above the absorption edge (1.610 eV) at the same base temperature of 7 K, the pump-probe signals are nearly identical. Only a positive response is seen, and the dynamics are much shorter lived with a lifetime on the order of 3 ps for both cases."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "temperature_evolution_negative_response",
      "statement": "With 1.476 eV pumping, the suppression in ΔE/E significantly weakens from 7 K to 70 K, is barely recognizable at 130 K, and becomes positive at room temperature.",
      "quantitative_form": "Qualitative: ΔE/E negative at 7 K, weaker at 70 K, barely at 130 K, positive at room temperature",
      "mechanism_ids": ["transient_response_lifetimes"],
      "control_names": ["temperature", "pump_photon_energy"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_E_over_E"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Pumping at 1.476 eV across temperatures from 7 K to room temperature.",
      "statistical_basis": "Set of pump-probe overlap scans at various temperatures.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 72,
          "line_end": 72,
          "support": "Figure 2(c) shows a set of pump-probe overlap scans at various temperatures across TN = 155 K ... With increasing temperature, the exciton peak red-shifts and broadens considerably at 70 K, before nearly disappearing at 130 K. Accordingly, the suppression in ΔE/E significantly weakens from 7 K to 70 K, and it is barely recognizable at 130 K. ... Consistently, a positive ΔE/E is seen at room temperature with 1.476 eV pumping, similar to the off-resonant case at 7 K."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "negative_delta_sigma1_peakI",
      "statement": "With resonant pumping at peak I at 7 K, Δσ1(ω) is positive at low energies and becomes negative between 6 and 10 meV at maximum response, and remains negative from 4 to 10 meV at later delays, while Δσ2(ω) is positive over the entire energy window.",
      "quantitative_form": "Δσ1(ω) < 0 for 4-10 meV at later delays; Δσ2(ω) > 0 over entire window",
      "mechanism_ids": ["drude_lorentz_fit"],
      "control_names": ["pump_photon_energy", "temperature"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_sigma_1", "delta_sigma_2"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["base_temperature_7K", "pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Resonant pumping at 1.476 eV at 7 K.",
      "statistical_basis": "Frequency-resolved measurements at various time delays.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "At the maximum response (Fig. 3(a)), Δσ1(ω) is positive at low energies and becomes negative in between 6 and 10 meV, while Δσ2(ω) is positive over the entire energy window. At later time delays, Δσ1(ω) remains negative from 4 to 10 meV, while Δσ2(ω) remains positive (Fig. 3(b-d))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "drude_response_peakII",
      "statement": "With pumping at peak II (1.494 eV) at 7 K, Δσ1(ω) remains positive over the entire energy window, decreasing at higher probe energies, while Δσ2(ω) peaks near 7 meV, characteristic of a free-carrier Drude response.",
      "quantitative_form": "Δσ1(ω) > 0 for all ω, decreasing with ω; Δσ2(ω) peaks near 7 meV",
      "mechanism_ids": ["drude_lorentz_fit"],
      "control_names": ["pump_photon_energy", "temperature"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_sigma_1", "delta_sigma_2"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["base_temperature_7K", "pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Pumping at 1.494 eV at 7 K.",
      "statistical_basis": "Frequency-resolved measurement.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 80,
          "line_end": 80,
          "support": "Figure 3(g) shows the transient change of the complex optical conductivity with pumping at peak II, which is about 20 meV above peak I. In this configuration, Δσ1(ω) remains positive over the entire energy window, decreasing at higher probe energies, while Δσ2(ω) peaks near 7 meV. This is characteristic of a free-carrier Drude response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "population_inversion_interpretation",
      "statement": "The negative Δσ1(ω) observed with resonant pumping at peak I is interpreted as stimulated emission from population inversion of intraexcitonic levels, formed by resonant excitation and probed by THz photons.",
      "quantitative_form": "Qualitative interpretation",
      "mechanism_ids": ["drude_lorentz_fit"],
      "control_names": ["pump_photon_energy"],
      "readout_names": ["delta_sigma_1"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["base_temperature_7K", "pump_polarization_a_axis", "probe_polarization_b_axis", "reflection_geometry"],
      "applicability": "Resonant pumping at 1.476 eV at 7 K.",
      "statistical_basis": "Comparison with other exciton systems and fluence dependence.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified",
      "depends_on": ["negative_delta_sigma1_peakI"],
      "anchors": [
        {
